diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7195abf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,327 @@
+# Helpdesk API
+Rails 8.1 API-only backend for the helpdesk SaaS. The frontend is a separate app.
+All endpoints live under `/api/v1` and speak JSON.
+
+## Setup
+
+Requires Ruby 4.0 (see `.ruby-version`) and PostgreSQL. `DATABASE_URL` configures the
+primary connection, for example:
+
+    export DATABASE_URL="postgresql:///helpdesk_development?host=/var/lib/postgresql"
+    bundle install
+    bin/rails db:prepare
+    bin/rails server            # Puma, on $PORT (default 3000)
+
+Tests run against a separate database derived from `DATABASE_URL` (`helpdesk_development`
+becomes `helpdesk_test`), or against `TEST_DATABASE_URL` if it is set:
+
+    bin/rails test
+
+## Configuration
+
+| Variable | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `DATABASE_URL` | none | PostgreSQL connection |
+| `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL` | `http://localhost:3001` | Base URL for verification and reset links. Also the only browser origin allowed by CORS. |
+| `HELPDESK_MAIL_FROM` | `support@example.test` | Sender address for outgoing email |
+| `HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN` | `support.example.test` | Domain that receives support mail. Business `acme` gets `acme@<domain>`. |
+| `RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD` | none | Password for the inbound mail relay (username `actionmailbox`). Unset, the relay answers 500. |
+| `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD` | `localhost`, `587`, none, none | SMTP delivery (production). Development writes mail to `tmp/mails/`. |
+| `SECRET_KEY_BASE` | none | Required by Rails in production, even though the API does not use cookies. |
+
+## Conventions
+
+- **Business header:** every endpoint except `POST /api/v1/account/create` needs
+  `X-Helpdesk-Account: <subdomain>`. The header selects the business, and a session token only works within the business that issued it.
+- **Sessions:** send `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
+- **Errors:** `{"error": {"code": "...", "message": "...", "details": {...}}}`. Common codes:
+  `account_required` (400), `parameter_missing` (400), `invalid_json` (400), `unauthorized` (401),
+  `invalid_credentials` (401), `email_not_verified` (403), `user_suspended` (403), `account_cancelled` (403),
+  `forbidden` (403), `account_not_found` (404), `validation_failed` (422), `invalid_token` (422),
+  `invalid_current_password` (422), `already_cancelled` / `not_cancelled` / `reactivation_expired` (422),
+  `already_accepted` / `already_revoked` / `already_closed` (422).
+  A field sent in the wrong shape, such as `"comment": "text"` where an object belongs, is also `validation_failed` (422).
+- **Timestamps** are ISO 8601 UTC. Record IDs are integers.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+| Method and path | Auth | Purpose | Success |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `POST /account/create` | none | Register a business and its owner (admin). Sends a verification email. | 201 `{account, user, message}` |
+| `GET /portal` | header | Public business name and subdomain. | 200 `{portal: {name, subdomain}}` |
+| `POST /sessions` | header | Sign in. The email must be verified. | 201 `{session: {id, token}, user, account}` |
+| `DELETE /session` | token | Sign out this session. | 204 |
+| `GET /account` | token | Full account, including cancellation status. | 200 `{account}` |
+| `POST /account/cancel` | owner | Cancel the business and revoke all its sessions. | 200 `{account}` |
+| `POST /account/reactivate` | owner | Reactivate within 90 days of cancellation. | 200 `{account}` |
+| `GET /me` | token | Current user. | 200 `{user}` |
+| `PATCH /me` | token | Update the name: `{"user":{"name":"Jo Smith"}}`. | 200 `{user}` |
+| `PUT /me/password` | token | `{"password_change":{"current_password":"...","password":"..."}}`. Signs out all sessions. | 200 `{message}` |
+| `POST /email_verifications` | header | `{"email_verification":{"email":"..."}}`. Emails a new link, replacing the old one. | 202 |
+| `POST /email_verifications/confirm` | header | `{"email_verification":{"token":"..."}}`. | 200 `{user}` |
+| `POST /password_resets` | header | `{"password_reset":{"email":"..."}}`. Emails a new reset link, replacing the old one. | 202 |
+| `POST /password_resets/confirm` | header | `{"password_reset":{"token":"...","password":"..."}}`. Signs out all sessions. | 200 `{message}` |
+| `POST /registrations` | header | Customer sign-up for the portal: `{"user":{"name":"...","email":"...","password":"..."}}`. Sends a verification email. The role is always `customer`. | 201 `{user, message}` |
+| `GET /requests` | token | The signed-in user's own requests, most recently active first. Filter with `?status=open\|pending\|solved\|closed`. Paginated. | 200 `{requests, count, page, per_page, next_page, previous_page}` |
+| `POST /requests` | token | Open a request: `{"request":{"subject":"...","comment":{"body":"..."}}}`. It starts `open`. | 201 `{request}` |
+| `GET /requests/:id` | token | One of your requests. Other people's requests return 404. | 200 `{request}` |
+| `PATCH /requests/:id` | token | Reply: `{"request":{"comment":{"body":"..."}}}`. Optionally add `"status":"solved"`. | 200 `{request}` |
+| `GET /requests/:id/comments` | token | Public comments on your request, oldest first. Paginated. | 200 `{comments, count, page, per_page, next_page, previous_page}` |
+| `GET /tickets` | staff | Every request in the business, most recently active first, with requester, group and priority. Filters combine: `?status=`, `?priority=normal\|high`, `?group_id=<id>\|unassigned`, `?assignee_id=<id>\|unassigned`, `?requester_id=<id>`, `?q=<text>`. Paginated. | 200 `{tickets, count, ...}` |
+| `GET /tickets/:id` | staff | One ticket with its requester. | 200 `{ticket}` |
+| `PATCH /tickets/:id` | staff | Reply, set status, group, assignee, and/or priority: `{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"...","public":true},"status":"pending","group_id":20,"priority":"high"}}`. Send at least one of `comment`, `status`, `group_id`, `assignee_id`, or `priority`. Group and assignee rules are under [Groups](#groups). | 200 `{ticket}` |
+| `GET /tickets/:id/comments` | staff | Every comment, including private notes, oldest first. Paginated. | 200 `{comments, count, ...}` |
+| `POST /tickets/:id/merge` | staff | Merge a duplicate into another ticket of the same customer: `{"merge":{"target_id":57}}`. The duplicate is closed. Optional notes: `source_comment` and `target_comment`, each `{"body":"...","public":false}`. See [Merging duplicates](#merging-duplicates). | 200 `{source, target}` |
+| `POST /invitations` | admin | Invite an admin or agent: `{"invitation":{"email":"...","role":"agent"}}`. Emails the link. | 201 `{invitation}` |
+| `GET /invitations` | admin | Every invitation by ID ascending. Filter with `?status=pending\|accepted\|revoked\|expired`. Paginated. | 200 `{invitations, count, ...}` |
+| `GET /invitations/:id` | admin | One invitation. | 200 `{invitation}` |
+| `POST /invitations/:id/resend` | admin | Emails a new link. The earlier link stops working and the expiry restarts. | 200 `{invitation}` |
+| `DELETE /invitations/:id` | admin | Revokes a pending or expired invitation. | 200 `{invitation}` |
+| `POST /invitation_previews` | header | What the acceptance screen shows: `{"invitation_preview":{"token":"..."}}`. | 200 `{invitation_preview}` |
+| `POST /invitation_acceptances` | header | Sets up the invitee: `{"invitation_acceptance":{"token":"...","name":"...","password":"..."}}`. The user is verified; no email is sent. | 201 `{user, account}` |
+| `GET /users` | admin | Everyone in the business by ID ascending. Filter with `?role=admin\|agent\|customer` and `?suspended=true\|false`. Paginated. | 200 `{users, count, ...}` |
+| `GET /users/:id` | admin | One user. | 200 `{user}` |
+| `PATCH /users/:id` | admin | Suspend or restore: `{"user":{"suspended":true}}`. | 200 `{user}` |
+| `GET /staff` | staff | Active admins and agents, by ID ascending, for assignment controls. Paginated. | 200 `{staff, count, ...}` |
+| `GET /groups` | staff | Every group by ID ascending. Paginated. | 200 `{groups, count, ...}` |
+| `GET /groups/:id` | staff | One group. | 200 `{group}` |
+| `POST /groups` | admin | Create a group: `{"group":{"name":"Billing"}}`. | 201 `{group}` |
+| `PATCH /groups/:id` | admin | Rename: `{"group":{"name":"Accounts"}}`. | 200 `{group}` |
+| `DELETE /groups/:id` | admin | Delete the group and its memberships. Its tickets become ungrouped and lose their assignees. | 204 |
+| `GET /group_memberships` | staff | Memberships by ID ascending. Filter with `?group_id=<id>` and/or `?user_id=<id>`. Paginated. | 200 `{group_memberships, count, ...}` |
+| `GET /group_memberships/:id` | staff | One membership. | 200 `{group_membership}` |
+| `POST /group_memberships` | admin | Add a staff member to a group: `{"group_membership":{"group_id":20,"user_id":12,"default":true}}`. `default` is optional. | 201 `{group_membership}` |
+| `PUT /group_memberships/:id/make_default` | admin | Make this group the person's default group. | 200 `{group_membership}` |
+| `DELETE /group_memberships/:id` | admin | Remove the person from the group. | 204 |
+
+Email requests answer the same way whether or not the address is registered.
+
+Links sent by email:
+
+- `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL/verify-email?account=<subdomain>&token=<token>`
+- `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL/reset-password?account=<subdomain>&token=<token>`
+
+- `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL/accept-invitation?account=<subdomain>&token=<token>`
+
+Staff means an `admin` or `agent` user. Customers who call `/tickets` get `403 forbidden`.
+Admins alone manage people: invitations and users. Agents and customers get `403 forbidden` there.
+
+### Requests and tickets
+
+- A request is also called a ticket. Customers see their own at `/requests`, and staff see all of them at `/tickets`.
+  Records come back in a `request` or `ticket` envelope. Staff responses include the requester as
+  `{"id":10,"name":"Avery","email":"avery@example.test"}`.
+- Statuses are `open`, `pending`, `solved`, and `closed`. A new request is `open`. `closed` is set only by merging
+  (see [Merging duplicates](#merging-duplicates)).
+- A customer reply reopens a `pending` or `solved` request. If the same update also marks it `solved`, it stays solved.
+  Customers may set only `solved`. Any other status is refused with `422`.
+- Staff can set `open`, `pending` or `solved`. A staff reply alone does not change the status. Staff cannot set `closed` directly.
+- Comments are public by default. Staff can send `"public": false` for a private note. Customers never see
+  private notes, in the comment list or anywhere else.
+- A public staff reply on an email ticket is emailed to the requester (see [Email](#email)). Other comments send nothing:
+  private notes, portal tickets, status changes and customer replies are not emailed.
+- **Assignment:** staff set `{"ticket":{"assignee_id":12}}` to assign a ticket, or `null` to clear it. The assignee must be
+  an active admin or agent of this business. Staff responses include `assignee_id` and `assignee: {id, name}`, or null.
+  Customers never see the assignment.
+- **Priority** is `normal` (the default) or `high`. Staff set it with `{"ticket":{"priority":"high"}}` and see it on
+  every staff ticket response. It is internal: customers never see it, and they cannot set it.
+- **Filtering staff lists.** Every filter is optional, and all the filters you send must match (AND):
+  - `status=open|pending|solved|closed` and `priority=normal|high`.
+  - `assignee_id=<id>` for one person's tickets, or `assignee_id=unassigned` for tickets nobody is working.
+  - `requester_id=<id>` for one customer's requests.
+  - `q=<text>` finds a case-insensitive match in subjects and public comments. Private notes are never searched.
+    `q` is literal text: `%`, `_`, and `\` match themselves, not wildcards. A blank `q` is ignored.
+  - A value outside the allowed values, or an ID that is not a positive whole number, returns `422 validation_failed`.
+  - Each ticket appears once, however many of its comments match, and `count` reflects the filtered total.
+- **Customer filtering.** Customers can filter their own requests with `?status=`, and nothing else. The staff-only filters
+  (`priority`, `assignee_id`, `requester_id`, and `q`) are ignored on `/requests`, so they reveal nothing about other requests.
+- **Internal activity does not change the customer's request.** A private note, a priority change, an assignment, or an assignment cleared
+  by suspension leaves `updated_at` and the request's place in the customer's list as they were. Only a public reply or
+  a status change counts as activity.
+- A private note is added through the same input: `{"ticket":{"comment":{"body":"Checking with billing.","public":false}}}`.
+- **`merged_into_id`** is on every request and ticket, for staff and customers alike. It is the ID of the ticket a closed
+  duplicate was merged into, and `null` for every other ticket.
+
+### Merging duplicates
+
+Staff merge a duplicate into another ticket of the same customer when several tickets are about one issue:
+
+    POST /api/v1/tickets/42/merge
+    {"merge":{"target_id":57,"source_comment":{"body":"Continuing in ticket 57.","public":true},
+               "target_comment":{"body":"Duplicate checked.","public":false}}}
+
+The response is `200 {source, target}`, both as staff see them. The rules are:
+
+- **The duplicate is closed.** Its status becomes `closed` and its `merged_into_id` becomes `57`. It stays readable: its
+  comments and reply address are unchanged, and both staff and the customer can list it with `?status=closed`.
+- **The surviving ticket is not changed** beyond its `updated_at`. Its status, assignee, and reply address stay as they
+  were, and no comments move to it.
+- **Notes.** Each side gets one note. `source_comment` goes on the duplicate and `target_comment` on the surviving ticket.
+  Both are private unless `"public": true`. A blank or missing `body` gets default text ("Merged into ticket 57." and
+  "Ticket 42 was merged into this ticket."). Notes are written by the staff member who merged, and merging sends no email,
+  even for a public note on an email ticket.
+- **Closed tickets take no edits.** Any staff update or customer reply to a closed ticket returns `422 already_closed`,
+  and nothing is saved. This covers replies, status, group, assignee and priority changes. Its own comments can still be read.
+- **Closed tickets take no email.** Mail to a closed ticket's reply address is bounced. It does not open a new ticket. Mail
+  to the surviving ticket's reply address works as before.
+- **Assignment cleanup still applies.** Suspending a user, removing them from a group, or deleting a group clears their
+  assignments on closed tickets too.
+- **Refused merges** return `422 validation_failed` with `details.target_id`, and nothing is saved. The target must be
+  another ticket of this business, from the same customer, and not closed. A duplicate that is already closed returns
+  `422 already_closed`.
+- **Later merges keep the original links.** If the surviving ticket is merged into a third ticket, it closes and points
+  at that ticket. The duplicate still points at the ticket it was first merged into.
+- **Atomic.** The merge and both notes are saved together, or not at all. A note that fails validation (for example, a body
+  over 10,000 characters) rejects the whole merge.
+
+## Email
+
+Customers can write to support from their mail client. Each business receives mail at `<subdomain>@<HELPDESK_SUPPORT_DOMAIN>`
+(`acme@support.example.test` by default). Every ticket also has a private reply address, `<subdomain>+<token>@<domain>`. A staff
+public reply on an email ticket is sent to the requester with `Reply-To` set to that address, so the requester's reply comes back
+to the same conversation.
+
+### Inbound relay
+
+Mail arrives through Action Mailbox's relay ingress. The SMTP server posts each raw message to it:
+
+    POST /rails/action_mailbox/relay/inbound_emails
+    Content-Type: message/rfc822
+    Authorization: Basic base64("actionmailbox:$RAILS_INBOUND_EMAIL_PASSWORD")
+
+It answers `204` once the message is recorded. `401` means the password is wrong, `415` means the content type is not `message/rfc822`.
+In test, routing happens inside the request. Elsewhere, routing is an Active Job (`ActionMailbox::RoutingJob`), so production needs a
+persistent job backend and a worker running it. The app ships without one, so configure one before taking live mail.
+
+### What happens to a delivery
+
+- **Business.** Every To, Cc and Bcc recipient on the support domain is considered. Recipients for businesses that do not exist
+  are ignored. The mail must then address exactly one business. Mail to several businesses, to none, or to a cancelled business
+  is rejected.
+- **Automated and list mail is rejected.** That covers `Auto-Submitted` (other than `no`), `List-*` headers,
+  `Precedence: bulk|list|junk|auto_reply`, `X-Autoreply`, `X-Autorespond`, bounce reports
+  (`multipart/report`), an empty `Return-Path`, and senders such as `mailer-daemon@`, `postmaster@` or `no-reply@`.
+- **Reply address.** Mail to a ticket's reply address is a reply on that ticket. Only the requester's address may do this. Any
+  other sender is rejected. A reply joins the conversation and reopens a `pending` or `solved` ticket. A reply to a closed
+  (merged) ticket's address is rejected and opens nothing. A reply to an unknown reply
+  address opens a new ticket.
+- **Plain support address.** A new message opens a ticket with the mail's subject, and its plain-text message as the first comment.
+  The subject is shortened to 255 characters and the message to 10,000. A missing subject becomes `(no subject)`. A message with
+  only HTML is converted to text.
+- **Customers.** The sender is matched by address. An existing customer is reused, and receiving mail does not verify anyone.
+  A new sender becomes an unverified customer and is sent the verification link. That customer has a random password, so after
+  confirming the link they set one with `POST /password_resets`, and then they can sign in. Mail from a staff member, or from a
+  suspended customer, is rejected.
+- **Deduplication.** The Message-ID of the email is stored on the comment it creates, and only one comment can hold a given
+  Message-ID. A redelivery of a message that was delivered creates nothing, and this holds even after the raw email is
+  incinerated. A rejected or failed delivery creates no comment, so a later retry of the same message is processed.
+- **Requester replies** are not emailed back. Staff replies go out only when the requester is not suspended.
+
+### Maintenance
+
+- Raw emails are kept for 30 days, then deleted (`config.action_mailbox.incinerate_after`). Processed inbound rows are deleted
+  with them.
+- A duplicate delivery leaves its raw email unattached. Purge those daily with `bin/rails helpdesk:purge_orphaned_blobs`.
+- Raw emails are stored under `storage/` in development and production.
+
+### Pagination
+
+Every list endpoint supports two styles. A request uses one of them.
+
+**Page numbers** take `page` (default 1) and `per_page` (default 25, maximum 100). Each list returns `count`
+(the total across all pages), `page`, `per_page`, `next_page`, and `previous_page`. The last two are `null`
+when there is no such page. A value that is not a positive whole number returns `422 validation_failed`.
+Counting the whole list gets slower as it grows, so for long histories use cursors.
+
+**Cursors** take `page[size]` (default 25, maximum 100). `page[after]=<cursor>` gives the records after a position,
+and `page[before]=<cursor>` gives the records before it. Send neither for the first page:
+
+    GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open
+    GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open&page[after]=<meta.after_cursor>
+
+A cursor page returns the records with `meta` and `links`, and no `count`:
+
+    {"tickets": [...],
+     "meta": {"has_more": true, "after_cursor": "...", "before_cursor": "..."},
+     "links": {"next": "https://.../api/v1/tickets?page%5Bafter%5D=...&page%5Bsize%5D=25&status=open", "prev": null}}
+
+- **Cursors for the first and last records.** `meta.after_cursor` is the cursor of the page's last record, and
+  `meta.before_cursor` is the cursor of its first record. A page with no records has `null` for both and `null` links.
+- **Links** lead to the neighbouring pages in either direction. They keep the filters and the page size, and are
+  `null` when there is no such page.
+- **`has_more`** says whether more records lie in the direction being browsed: after the page for `page[after]`
+  and the first page, and before it for `page[before]`.
+- **Cursors are opaque.** Each one is signed and belongs to one business and one endpoint. A cursor from another list,
+  or one that was altered, returns `422 validation_failed` under `page[after]` or `page[before]`. Filters are not part
+  of a cursor, so a client can change them between pages. Cursors stay valid until the server's `SECRET_KEY_BASE`
+  changes. After that, start again from the first page.
+- **Refused combinations** return `422 validation_failed`: `page[...]` with `page` or `per_page`, both `page[after]` and
+  `page[before]`, and an unknown `page[...]` option. A `page[size]` that is not a positive whole number is also refused.
+
+**Stability while others work.** A list keeps its order: tickets and requests by most recent activity, comments oldest
+first, and everything else by ID. A cursor names a position in that order, not a row count, so records added while you
+browse do not shift or repeat the pages you read. Activity moves a ticket to the top of the list. A ticket that changes
+before you reach it can therefore be missed going forward, and can reappear going back, just as it would in the list
+itself. Deleting a record does not break a cursor.
+
+## Invitations and people
+
+Admins invite people to the business as `admin` or `agent`. Customers join through registration instead.
+
+- The emailed link is `HELPDESK_FRONTEND_URL/accept-invitation?account=<subdomain>&token=<token>`. Links expire after
+  7 days and are single use. Resending replaces the link.
+- **Statuses** are `pending`, `accepted`, `revoked`, and `expired`. Acceptance and revocation are final. Expiry is based
+  on time, and an expired invitation can still be resent or revoked. Accepted and revoked invitations cannot be changed.
+- **One live invitation per address.** An address cannot be invited while it has a pending invitation or already
+  belongs to a user of the business. Expired and revoked invitations do not count.
+- **Customer registration is refused** for an address with a pending invitation (`422 validation_failed`).
+- **Accepting** does not require a password reset or another email. Accepting creates a verified user with the invited
+  role, and the user signs in with `POST /sessions` as usual.
+- **Previews** return `{invitation_preview: {email, role, business_name, subdomain, expires_at}}`. An unknown, expired,
+  used, or revoked token returns `422 invalid_token`, and all of those look the same.
+- **Suspending** a user signs them out of every session, clears their ticket assignments, and blocks sign-in with
+  `403 user_suspended`. Restoring them with `{"user":{"suspended":false}}` allows sign-in again. Suspension does not change
+  anyone's requests. Their group memberships are kept for when they are restored.
+- **The last active admin** cannot be suspended (`422 validation_failed`). Suspended admins do not count as active.
+- Only `suspended` can be changed through `PATCH /users/:id`. Roles are fixed when the user is created.
+
+## Groups
+
+Groups organize the team's work, such as Billing or Technical Support. Admins create, rename and delete them, and staff
+browse them. Groups never hide tickets: every staff member sees every ticket, and a group only says who works on what.
+Customers are not in groups and never see a ticket's group.
+
+- **Names** are trimmed and must be unique within the business, ignoring case (`422 validation_failed` otherwise).
+- **Members** are staff (admins and agents), one membership per group. A person can be in several groups. A suspended
+  person keeps their memberships.
+- **Default group.** Each staff member with memberships has exactly one default. Their first membership is the default,
+  whatever `default` says. A later membership with `"default": true`, or `PUT /group_memberships/:id/make_default`,
+  moves the default to that group. Removing the default makes the remaining membership with the lowest ID the default.
+- **Tickets** have an optional group, shown to staff as `group_id` and `group: {id, name}`. The inbox filters with
+  `?group_id=<id>` or `?group_id=unassigned`.
+- **Assignees** must belong to the ticket's group. Assigning someone else is refused with `422 validation_failed`.
+  Assigning someone to a ticket with no group gives the ticket that person's default group, if they have one. A person
+  with no groups can still be assigned, and the ticket stays ungrouped.
+- **Moving** a ticket to another group clears its assignee if that person is not in the new group.
+- **Clearing the assignee** (`"assignee_id": null`) leaves the group in place. **Clearing the group**
+  (`"group_id": null`) also clears the assignee. Clearing the group and assigning someone in the same update is refused.
+- **Removing a membership** clears that person's assignments on tickets in that group. **Deleting a group** ungroups its
+  tickets and clears their assignees. Other memberships are not affected.
+- Group changes are internal, like assignments and priority, so they do not change a ticket's `updated_at`.
+
+## Account lifecycle
+
+- Only the owner (the admin who registered the business) can cancel or reactivate.
+- Cancelling revokes every session in the business. Ordinary access stops: the portal, sign-in for
+  other users, and everything except the endpoints above return `403 account_cancelled`.
+- The owner can still sign in while cancelled, but only until 90 days have passed, and only to reactivate.
+- Accounts still cancelled after 90 days are deleted with all their users and sessions. Run this daily:
+
+      bin/rails helpdesk:purge_cancelled_accounts
+
+## Security notes
+
+- Only SHA-256 digests of session, verification, and reset tokens are stored.
+- Verification links expire after 3 days and reset links after 2 hours. Both are single use.
+- Passwords need at least 8 characters.
+- Only SHA-256 digests of invitation tokens are stored. Invitation links expire after 7 days and are single use.
